Item 6: hardware safety on the J516C (Marcelo)

Uncommitted: item6.diff (a doc-comment correction in pools/growth.rs) on top of 40b47534 (series
head, pushed). Not committed yet in agent-docs: the test tool tools/pb-idle-check/ (C source,
3 shaders, build.sh, pb-idle-run.sh) and results/20261023-r182-item6-pb-idle/ (README.md and
run.txt). Please read that README; it is the write-up.

FINDINGS
(a) A mid-client re-init is guarded only by time: ab98a724 says a no-idle re-init between two
renders of one client faulted at the heap base, and the 50 ms path does not know whose render
comes next. Asked for: one process with a heavy scene (> 2047 blocks), idle 60 ms, render, idle
1.1 s, render, plus the fault count and pb stats.
(b) A level-2 reclaim frees posted blocks before the re-init. Asked for: compute right after a
level-2 reclaim, before the next render's InitBM, plus the fault count.
Also: write up what we know about why the no-idle re-init faulted.

HARDWARE RUN (all on this boot)
- Package built on the M3 from 40b47534 (build-stack177.sh, BUILD-OK; the vmlinux holds the
  item 3 log string). It was installed with pacman -U and booted with
  `m3-accept r182-item6 --image ~/m3-images/t35/boot.bin.pr177`. Verdict FAIL only on
  crtc_file/presents, which is expected with #177's display driver. Native checks ran
  (compute 8399, vk 5632, gl 6523, zink 4171 runs in 5 s), kmscube 120.03 fps, edid_bytes 128.
  Record: results/20261023-093208-r182-item6-accept/.
- Tool: tools/pb-idle-check, one Vulkan device per process, so one VM and one client. It was
  built in the M3's Mesa chroot (cc -Wall -Wextra, no warnings). Calibration on the #363 kernel:
  350000 triangles grow about 2550 blocks from 64 in one render (above 2047, below the 3072
  firmware maximum); 200000 grow about 1440.
- Sequences (pb-idle-run.sh, desktop stopped; output in run.txt; the boot's dmesg is in
  ~/m3-private/r182-item6-dmesg.txt on the M3):
  a1 R 60 R 1100 R (350k); a2 R 60 R 60 R 1100 R 60 R 1100 R; a3 the same as a1 with 200k;
  a4 R 60 x6 (6 consecutive client-boundary re-inits in one client, the r2c failure shape);
  c six heavy processes back to back; b1/b2 R 1100 C 10 C 100 C R (350k/200k);
  b3 R 1100 C 1100 C R C.
- RESULT: 0 GPU faults, recoveries or hangs. All 8 sequences report `faults: 0`; the only
  boot-wide pattern match is "raid6: using neon recovery algorithm". Every readback and every
  compute check passed. Kernel events: 15 client-boundary re-inits at 51 ms (6 consecutive in
  a4), 5 frees of the kept backing with the re-init pending (20 chunks each, at 1027-1058 ms),
  2 level-2 idle reclaims (11 chunks), and 8 computes between a level-2 free and the next InitBM.
  pb counters ended at "requests 5932 grown 59320 refused 0 bad 0 waits 0 refills 264
  reclaims 24 freed 244".
- So (a) shows no problem, and per the task I did NOT implement the "next render from a
  different VM" keying. The r2c analysis below also shows that keying would not have avoided
  the r2c faults.

WHY THE NO-IDLE RE-INIT FAULTED (r2c private log, details in the README)
- Every r2c no-idle re-init was at a client switch: ctx 40->43, 43->46, 49->52, 52->55,
  58->61, 61->2, with a new VM for each dEQP run. The faulting render was the NEW client's first
  render. The faults came on the second no-idle re-init in a row (43->46, 52->55, 61->2); the
  first one after a long idle or after a recovery did not fault.
- Signature: 0.4 ms after the InitBM, before any growth request, units 0x15 and 0x25 wrote to
  unmapped addresses at heap base +0x100/+0x500/+0x700 (block 0, posted and backed, and mapped
  by map_pb() at publication), in the new client's ctx. Event type 4 followed, then recovery,
  and the client lost its device.
- Cause unknown (firmware internal). Supported conclusions: it is not a host mapping gap, and it
  needs two back-to-back no-idle InitBMs; a recovery or a long idle in between clears the
  condition. Leading hypothesis, not shown: the firmware's own PB page recycling after a scene
  has no completion signal, and an InitBM that lands during it corrupts what the next InitBM
  starts from. 50 ms is an empirical margin, not a proven quiescence point. r2d never ran
  consecutive re-inits; this run had 15 at 51 ms, 6 of them consecutive, with no fault.
- Compute after a level-2 reclaim: a compute-only prefix has no TA/3D work and does not tile
  into the PB. 8 computes ran in the window (about 60 ms to 1.2 s after the free) without a fault.
  Firmware background PB work in that window rests on the same empirical argument, not on an
  interface guarantee.

CODE FIX (item6.diff): a factual correction found by this analysis
Our own text says the no-idle fault happened "between two renders of one client"; the r2c log
shows client switches. Corrected:
- pools/growth.rs, drain_reinit_due() doc: "A re-initialization with no idle time faulted at the
  heap base (at a client switch, the second in a row with no recovery between); with 50 ms of
  idle none did, also between two renders of one client."
- Planned message change in 14cc13d9 "re-initialize the parameter buffer at a client
  boundary": replace "50 ms is longer than the gaps between one client's renders: a
  re-initialization with no idle time, between two renders of one client, faulted at the heap
  base." with "50 ms is longer than the gaps between one client's renders. On the J516C a
  re-initialization with no idle time faulted at the heap base, at a client switch, the second
  time in a row with no recovery between; with 50 ms of idle none faulted, also between two
  renders of one client 60 ms apart." Fixup target: 14cc13d9 (the commit that introduces
  drain_reinit_due() and that text).
